      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, physical appearance and demographics significantly influence jury perceptions of credibility, often triggering unconscious biases such as associating suits with authority or hoodies with danger. Unlike the specific evidence, these visual cues can subconsciously shape whether a witness is viewed as credible or unreliable regardless of their actual accuracy. This phenomenon, known as the \"halo effect,\" is a critical factor that legal theory warns against, even though it remains a powerful driver of decision-making in mock trials and real courtrooms.",
      "source": "target",
